A direct “random graph plus planted path” would leak the path. I’m instead using a barrier construction: a shortest Token Jumping sequence exists exactly when an auxiliary regular graph can be 3-coloured. The planted colour classes are sampled first, every auxiliary vertex has the same degree, and every colour option has the same degree in the reconfiguration graph; this removes the obvious plant/decoy degree signal while preserving an NP-hard search core. I’m sweeping density now because overly sparse instances admit greedy colouring and overly dense planted instances can reveal their partition.
