@@ -0,0 +1,230 @@
+# Verified generator for arXiv:2408.04743
+
+This module turns Cranston, Mühlenthaler, and Peyrille's [*A simple quadratic kernel for Token Jumping on surfaces*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.04743) into a bounded-witness search task. A solver receives a compactly defined graph, an independent start set, an independent goal set, and an exact jump budget. It must return the ordered `(removed vertex, added vertex)` jumps. `verify` cheaply replays every jump, checks occupancy and graph independence exactly, and compares the final set with the goal; it never reads the planted answer.
+
+## Why the family is hard
+
+Section 2 of the paper fixes the precise rule used here: each state is an independent set of the same size and consecutive states remove exactly one vertex and add exactly one vertex. The construction puts `n+1` tokens on start vertices `S`, makes every `S` vertex adjacent to every goal vertex `T`, and provides three colour-choice vertices for each vertex of an auxiliary graph `H`. A path of exactly `2n+1` jumps can contain only one `S -> T` jump. Equality in this lower bound forces the other `n` tokens through exactly one choice vertex for every vertex of `H`; choice edges make those vertices an independent set exactly when they describe a proper 3-colouring. Conversely, every proper 3-colouring gives such a path. Thus the bounded search relation contains 3-COLOURING and has no known polynomial-time or closed-form solver.
+
+Section 1 reports unrestricted Token Jumping as PSPACE-complete even on subcubic planar bounded-bandwidth graphs, and W[1]-hard when parameterised by token count plus jump count. It also warns about easy parameter regimes: FPT on planar and `K_{3,t}`-free graphs for small token count, and kernels on bounded-degree, bounded-degeneracy, and fixed-surface graphs. Theorem 1 gives the paper's `O(g²+gk+k²)` kernel. This generator stays outside those small-parameter promises: `k=n+1` and `ell=2n+1` both grow, and no fixed-genus promise is made.
+
+The answer is sampled first. Three equal colour classes, their visible colour names, and every jump order are shuffled; only afterward is `H` drawn as edge-disjoint perfect matchings between class pairs. Every `H` vertex has degree six, and every planted or decoy colour-choice vertex has the same degree, removing the first-order outlier signal.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | `n` | TJ vertices | tokens | jumps | auxiliary edges | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| easy | 18 | 92 | 19 | 37 | 54 | Oracle-solvable; example only |
+| medium | 180 | 902 | 181 | 361 | 540 | **Shipping** |
+| hard | 270 | 1,352 | 271 | 541 | 810 | Available escalation |
+
+A retired `n=90` rung was rejected even though three LLM oracles failed it: the randomized-DSATUR attack solved 6/8 local seeds. The shipping window was therefore moved to `n=180`, where the same attack solved 0/8.
+
+## Worked easy example (`seed=0`)
+
+<details>
+<summary>Complete rendered instance</summary>
+
+```text
+Bounded-length Token Jumping
+
+A graph here is finite, undirected, and simple. An independent set is a
+set of vertices with no graph edge between any two of them. A token state
+is an independent set. One token jump removes one occupied vertex r and
+adds one different, currently unoccupied vertex a; the state after the
+jump must again be independent.
+
+The graph has vertex IDs 0 through 91, inclusive.
+There are 19 indistinguishable tokens.
+Start vertices S are 0 through 18, inclusive.
+Goal vertices T are 19 through 37, inclusive.
+For each auxiliary vertex v in 0..17 and colour c in 0..2,
+the colour-choice vertex X(v,c) has ID 38 + 3*v + c.
+
+These rules define every graph edge; there are no other edges:
+1. Every S vertex is adjacent to every T vertex.
+2. For each v, X(v,0), X(v,1), and X(v,2) are pairwise adjacent.
+3. For each auxiliary edge 'u v' listed below and each c in 0..2,
+   X(u,c) is adjacent to X(v,c).
+
+Auxiliary edges (one undirected edge per line, endpoints are 0-indexed):
